title open problem 3: what a directory rename costs full-path naming

# Section 15, open problem 3.  Names are full paths, so renaming one
# directory renames every name beneath it.  Green evidence evaporates
# wholesale for the whole subtree, and red -- dnode identity -- is the
# only thing left holding the descendants together.
#
# carried/ shows that working.  Off-of renames paper/ to manuscript/;
#   every file inside changes its name and keeps its dnode, so not one
#   green edge survives on the base-to-off-of face down there and red
#   pairs all of them anyway.  Onto's edit to body.tex follows the
#   rename and lands at the new path.  This is the case people mean
#   when they say full-path naming is fine.
#
# lost/ shows the price.  Off-of renames notes/ to journal/ AND saves
#   todo.md the way an editor saves it, through a temp file renamed
#   over the target -- so that one file changed BOTH its name (because
#   its parent moved) and its dnode (because the editor swapped it).
#   Neither edge survives, so the file that was edited on onto looks
#   deleted, and what off-of wrote looks newly created.  Result: a
#   lineage conflict over a file nobody deleted.
#
#   Under parent-relative naming -- a name as (parent dnode, leaf) --
#   the leaf "todo.md" under parent dnode 20 is the same name on both
#   sides, green survives the directory rename, and this decides.  The
#   trap the audit found is in the same breath: parent dnodes are only
#   comparable across trees through red, so green would come to depend
#   on red, and the names-only fallback world would be gone.

tree base
  1-1: /
    2-100: carried/
      10-100: paper/
        11-100: body.tex = body-v1
        12-100: refs.bib = refs-v1
    3-100: lost/
      20-100: notes/
        21-100: todo.md = todo-v1
        22-100: keep.md = keep-v1

tree onto
  # Onto edits one file in each folder and touches nothing else.
  1-1: /
    2-100: carried/
      10-100: paper/
        11-100: body.tex = body-v2
        12-100: refs.bib = refs-v1
    3-100: lost/
      20-100: notes/
        21-100: todo.md = todo-v2
        22-100: keep.md = keep-v1

tree offof
  1-1: /
    2-100: carried/
      # renamed by dnode: 10 is still 10, and so are 11 and 12.
      10-100: manuscript/
        11-100: body.tex = body-v1
        12-100: refs.bib = refs-v1
    3-100: lost/
      # renamed by dnode as well, but todo.md was saved through a temp
      # file on the way, so it lost its dnode at the same moment the
      # rename took its name away.
      20-100: journal/
        221-300: todo.md = todo-v1a
        22-100: keep.md = keep-v1

# --- expected result, generated by gen-expected.js ---
expect conflict lineage
expect quarantined /lost/notes

tree expected
  1-1: /
    2-100: carried
      10-100: manuscript
        11-100: body.tex = body-v2
        12-100: refs.bib = refs-v1
    3-100: lost
      20-100: notes
        22-100: keep.md = keep-v1
        21-100: todo.md = todo-v2
